<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="midterm-review-f26">
<title>Midterm Review: Fall 2026</title>
<introduction>
<p>This review covers evaluating functions, composition and inverses, average rate of change, linear functions, and circular functions. Show your work on each problem.</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 1: evaluating a function                              -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-evaluate">
<title>Problem 1: Evaluating a Function</title>
<introduction>
<p>Let <m>f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x + 5</m>.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Find <m>f(-2)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>f(-2) = 2(-2)^2 - 3(-2) + 5 = 8 + 6 + 5 = 19</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="2.5in">
<statement>
<p>Find <m>f(2x - 1)</m> and simplify.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Replace every <m>x</m> with <m>2x - 1</m>, then expand.
<md>
<mrow>f(2x - 1) \amp= 2(2x - 1)^2 - 3(2x - 1) + 5</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2(4x^2 - 4x + 1) - 6x + 3 + 5</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 8x^2 - 8x + 2 - 6x + 8</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 8x^2 - 14x + 10</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>As a check, <m>x = 0</m> gives <m>10</m>, and <m>f(-1) = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 2: tables, composition, inverses                      -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-tables">
<title>Problem 2: Functions Given by a Table</title>
<introduction>
<p>The table below gives some values of the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>.</p>
<tabular halign="center">
<col right="major"/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>

<task workspace="0.75in">
<statement>
<p>Find <m>f(1)</m> and <m>g(0)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>f(1) = 2</m> and <m>g(0) = 3</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Find <m>(f \circ g)(0)</m>, <m>(g \circ f)(-1)</m>, and <m>f(f(2))</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Work from the inside out.</p>
<p><m>(f \circ g)(0) = f(g(0)) = f(3) = 4</m>.</p>
<p><m>(g \circ f)(-1) = g(f(-1)) = g(1) = 2</m>.</p>
<p><m>f(f(2)) = f(0) = -1</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Which of <m>f</m> or <m>g</m> has an inverse? Explain how you know. For the function that has an inverse, find its inverse evaluated at <m>-1</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>f</m> has an inverse because every input gives a different output. <m>g</m> does not, since <m>g(-1) = 2</m> and <m>g(1) = 2</m>; two different inputs give the same output.</p>
<p>Since <m>f(0) = -1</m>, we have <m>f^{-1}(-1) = 0</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 3: four graphs                                        -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-graphs">
<title>Problem 3: Functions and Inverses from Graphs</title>
<introduction>
<p>Use the four graphs below to answer the questions.</p>
<sbsgroup widths="45% 45%">
<sidebyside>
<image>
<shortdescription>Graph (a): an upward opening parabola.</shortdescription>
<description><p>An upward opening parabola with vertex at (0, -2), crossing the x-axis near -1.4 and 1.4.</p></description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mr-graph-a">
<diagram dimensions="(220,220)" margins="5">
<definition>f(x) = x^2 - 2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
<grid/>
<axes/>
<graph function="f" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="2"/>
<label anchor="(-3.3,3.3)">(a)</label>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>Graph (b): a circle centered at the origin.</shortdescription>
<description><p>A circle centered at the origin with radius 2.5.</p></description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mr-graph-b">
<diagram dimensions="(220,220)" margins="5">
<coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
<grid/>
<axes/>
<circle center="(0,0)" radius="2.5" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="2"/>
<label anchor="(-3.3,3.3)">(b)</label>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
<sidebyside>
<image>
<shortdescription>Graph (c): a decreasing line.</shortdescription>
<description><p>A straight line with negative slope, crossing the y-axis at 1 and the x-axis at 2.</p></description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mr-graph-c">
<diagram dimensions="(220,220)" margins="5">
<definition>h(x) = -0.5*x + 1</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
<grid/>
<axes/>
<graph function="h" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="2"/>
<label anchor="(-3.3,3.3)">(c)</label>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<image>
<shortdescription>Graph (d): a parabola opening to the right.</shortdescription>
<description><p>A parabola opening to the right with vertex at (-2, 0), crossing the y-axis near -1.4 and 1.4.</p></description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mr-graph-d">
<diagram dimensions="(220,220)" margins="5">
<definition>k(t) = (t^2 - 2, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
<grid/>
<axes/>
<parametric-curve function="k" domain="(-2.4,2.4)" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="2"/>
<label anchor="(-3.3,3.3)">(d)</label>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</sbsgroup>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Which graphs are <em>not</em> functions? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Graphs (b) and (d) are not functions. Each fails the vertical line test; for example, the line <m>x = 0</m> crosses each of them twice.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Which graphs are functions that have an inverse? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Graph (c) is a function with an inverse. It passes the vertical line test, and every horizontal line crosses it exactly once, so it passes the horizontal line test.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Which graphs are functions that do <em>not</em> have an inverse? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Graph (a) is a function, but it fails the horizontal line test. For example, the line <m>y = 2</m> crosses it at <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 2</m>, so it has no inverse.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 4: average rate of change of a formula                -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-aroc-formula">
<title>Problem 4: Average Rate of Change of a Function</title>
<introduction>
<p>Let <m>f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 1</m>.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1.75in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f</m> on the interval <m>[1, 4]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>f(1) = 1 - 4 + 1 = -2</m> and <m>f(4) = 16 - 16 + 1 = 1</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\frac{f(4) - f(1)}{4 - 1} \amp= \frac{1 - (-2)}{3} = 1</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.75in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f</m> on the interval <m>[-1, 2]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>f(-1) = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6</m> and <m>f(2) = 4 - 8 + 1 = -3</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\frac{f(2) - f(-1)}{2 - (-1)} \amp= \frac{-3 - 6}{3} = -3</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 5: average rate of change from a table in context     -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-aroc-table">
<title>Problem 5: Average Rate of Change in Context</title>
<introduction>
<p>A cup of hot tea is left to cool on a counter. The table gives the temperature <m>T</m> of the tea, in degrees Fahrenheit, <m>t</m> minutes after it was poured.</p>
<tabular halign="center">
<col right="major"/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<col/>
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m> (minutes)</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>10</m></cell>
<cell><m>15</m></cell>
<cell><m>20</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>T(t)</m> (<m>^\circ\text{F}</m>)</cell>
<cell><m>180</m></cell>
<cell><m>152</m></cell>
<cell><m>132</m></cell>
<cell><m>118</m></cell>
<cell><m>108</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>T</m> from <m>t = 0</m> to <m>t = 10</m>. Include units. Then write a sentence explaining what your answer means in this context.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>\frac{132 - 180}{10 - 0} = \frac{-48}{10} = -4.8</m> degrees Fahrenheit per minute.</p>
<p>Over the first 10 minutes, the tea cooled by an average of 4.8 degrees per minute.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>T</m> from <m>t = 10</m> to <m>t = 20</m>. Include units. Then write a sentence explaining what your answer means in this context.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>\frac{108 - 132}{20 - 10} = \frac{-24}{10} = -2.4</m> degrees Fahrenheit per minute.</p>
<p>Between 10 and 20 minutes after it was poured, the tea cooled by an average of 2.4 degrees per minute.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Compare your two answers. What do they tell you about how the tea is cooling?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The tea is still cooling, but more slowly as time goes on; it lost about half as many degrees per minute in the second 10 minutes as in the first.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 6: line through two points and its inverse            -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-line-inverse">
<title>Problem 6: A Line Through Two Points</title>
<introduction>
<p>A linear function <m>f</m> passes through the points <m>(-2, 7)</m> and <m>(4, -2)</m>.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Write an equation for <m>f</m> in point-slope form.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The slope is <m>m = \frac{-2 - 7}{4 - (-2)} = \frac{-9}{6} = -\frac{3}{2}</m>. Using the point <m>(-2, 7)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>y - 7 \amp= -\frac{3}{2}(x + 2)</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Write an equation for <m>f</m> in slope-intercept form.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Distribute and solve for <m>y</m>.
<md>
<mrow>y - 7 \amp= -\frac{3}{2}x - 3</mrow>
<mrow>y \amp= -\frac{3}{2}x + 4</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>As a check, <m>f(4) = -6 + 4 = -2</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.75in">
<statement>
<p>Find a formula for <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Swap <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, then solve for <m>y</m>.
<md>
<mrow>x \amp= -\frac{3}{2}y + 4</mrow>
<mrow>x - 4 \amp= -\frac{3}{2}y</mrow>
<mrow>y \amp= -\frac{2}{3}(x - 4) = -\frac{2}{3}x + \frac{8}{3}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>So <m>f^{-1}(x) = -\frac{2}{3}x + \frac{8}{3}</m>. As a check, <m>f(4) = -2</m> and <m>f^{-1}(-2) = \frac{4}{3} + \frac{8}{3} = 4</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 7: linear model from rate and initial value           -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-linear-rate">
<title>Problem 7: A Draining Tank</title>
<introduction>
<p>A tank holds <m>450</m> gallons of water. A valve is opened, and water drains out at a constant rate of <m>15</m> gallons per minute. Let <m>W(t)</m> be the amount of water in the tank, in gallons, <m>t</m> minutes after the valve is opened.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Find a formula for <m>W(t)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The initial value is <m>450</m> and the rate of change is <m>-15</m> gallons per minute, so <m>W(t) = 450 - 15t</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Find <m>W(12)</m> and explain what it means in this context.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>W(12) = 450 - 15(12) = 270</m>. After 12 minutes, there are 270 gallons of water left in the tank.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>How long does it take for the tank to empty?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Set <m>W(t) = 0</m>: <m>450 - 15t = 0</m>, so <m>t = 30</m>. The tank is empty after 30 minutes.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 8: linear model from two points in context            -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-linear-points">
<title>Problem 8: Cab Fare</title>
<introduction>
<p>A cab company charges a fixed fee plus a set amount per mile. A <m>4</m>-mile ride costs <m>\$13.50</m>, and a <m>10</m>-mile ride costs <m>\$27.00</m>. Let <m>C(m)</m> be the cost, in dollars, of an <m>m</m>-mile ride.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1.75in">
<statement>
<p>Find a formula for <m>C(m)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The slope is <m>\frac{27.00 - 13.50}{10 - 4} = \frac{13.50}{6} = 2.25</m>. Then <m>C(m) = 2.25m + b</m>, and using <m>C(4) = 13.50</m>,
<md>
<mrow>13.50 \amp= 2.25(4) + b</mrow>
<mrow>b \amp= 4.50</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>So <m>C(m) = 2.25m + 4.50</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Write a sentence explaining what the slope means in this context. Include units.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Each additional mile adds <m>\$2.25</m> to the cost of the ride.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>What does the vertical intercept of <m>C</m> mean in this context? How much does a <m>15</m>-mile ride cost?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The vertical intercept, <m>\$4.50</m>, is the fixed fee charged for every ride before any miles are driven. A 15-mile ride costs <m>C(15) = 2.25(15) + 4.50 = \$38.25</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 9: features of a circular function graph              -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-circular-graph">
<title>Problem 9: Reading a Circular Function Graph</title>
<introduction>
<p>The graph of a circular function <m>y = f(x)</m> is shown below.</p>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>A wave that oscillates between y = 1 and y = 5 for x from 0 to 12.</shortdescription>
<description><p>A smooth wave on a grid. It starts at the point (0, 1), rises to a maximum at (3, 5), falls to a minimum at (6, 1), rises to a maximum at (9, 5), and falls back to (12, 1).</p></description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mr-sinusoid">
<diagram dimensions="(420,240)" margins="5">
<definition>f(x) = -2*cos(pi*x/3) + 3</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,13,6.5)">
<grid/>
<axes/>
<graph function="f" domain="(0,12)" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="2"/>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Find the maximum value and the minimum value of <m>f</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The maximum value is <m>5</m> (at <m>x = 3</m> and <m>x = 9</m>). The minimum value is <m>1</m> (at <m>x = 0</m>, <m>6</m>, and <m>12</m>).</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Find the equation of the midline and the amplitude.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The midline is halfway between the max and min: <m>y = \frac{5 + 1}{2} = 3</m>. The amplitude is the distance from the midline to the max: <m>\frac{5 - 1}{2} = 2</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Find the period.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The graph goes from one minimum at <m>x = 0</m> to the next at <m>x = 6</m>, so the period is <m>6</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- Problem 10: circular model from context                       -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<page>
<exercise xml:id="mr-circular-model">
<title>Problem 10: A Ferris Wheel</title>
<introduction>
<p>This problem does not include a picture. Draw one before you start.</p>
<p>A Ferris wheel has a diameter of <m>50</m> feet, and its center is <m>30</m> feet above the ground. The wheel makes one full turn every <m>8</m> minutes. You board at the lowest point of the wheel at time <m>t = 0</m>. Let <m>h(t)</m> be your height above the ground, in feet, after <m>t</m> minutes.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Find the amplitude, midline, and period of <m>h</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The amplitude is the radius, <m>25</m> feet. The midline is the height of the center, <m>y = 30</m>. The period is <m>8</m> minutes.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Should the model start with <m>\sin</m>, <m>\cos</m>, <m>-\sin</m>, or <m>-\cos</m>? Explain your choice.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-\cos</m>. At <m>t = 0</m> you are at the lowest point, and <m>-\cos</m> is the one that starts at its minimum.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Write a formula for <m>h(t)</m>. Then find your height after <m>2</m> minutes.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>With period <m>8</m>, <m>B = \frac{2\pi}{8} = \frac{\pi}{4}</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>h(t) \amp= -25\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{4}t\right) + 30</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p><m>h(2) = -25\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) + 30 = 0 + 30 = 30</m> feet. This makes sense; after a quarter turn you are level with the center.</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Suppose instead that at <m>t = 0</m> you are level with the center of the wheel and moving upward. Which of <m>\sin</m>, <m>\cos</m>, <m>-\sin</m>, or <m>-\cos</m> would you use now? Write the new formula.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>\sin</m>, since it starts on the midline and increases. The new formula is <m>h(t) = 25\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}t\right) + 30</m>.</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

</worksheet>